# Milestone 3 Lesson Overviews

## Lesson 6: The AI Credibility Guide

#### Lesson 6 is a **Develop & Critique** lesson. Students synthesize their investigations into AI-generated outputs and transform their findings into a clear, visual **Trust Meter** for their **Responsible AI Use Guide**.

### 📝 **Lesson Overview**
<p style="font-size: 18px; font-weight: bold; background-color: #F5F7F8; padding: 10px; border-left: 5px solid #8148B2; display: inline-block; width: 100%">
<a href="https://studio.code.org/courses/aid-thinking-critically-about-ai-2025/units/1/lessons/6" target="blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
Access Lesson 6: The AI Credibility Guide
</a>
</p>

<!-- Warm Up -->
<details style="background-color: #ececec; padding: 12px; margin-top: 15px; margin-bottom: 15px; border-radius: 6px;">
<summary style="font-size: 13pt; font-weight: bold;">
Warm Up (5 minutes)
</summary>
<div style="margin-top: 12px; font-size: 13pt; line-height: 1.4;">
<p style="font-size: 13pt">
Students revisit their previous explorations and mini-lessons, sharing one surprising pattern they noticed about AI credibility.
</p>
<p style="font-size: 13pt">
This discussion prepares students to move from isolated observations to shared, actionable guidance about when to trust AI and when to verify.
</p>
</div>
</details>

<!-- Activity -->
<details style="background-color: #ececec; padding: 12px; margin-bottom: 15px; border-radius: 6px;">
<summary style="font-size: 13pt; font-weight: bold;">
Activity (35 minutes)
</summary>
<div style="margin-top: 12px; font-size: 13pt; line-height: 1.4;">
<p style="font-size: 14pt"><b>🔄 Jigsaw Synthesis</b></p>

<p style="font-size: 13pt">
Students first meet in <strong>Expert Groups</strong> based on their Lesson 5 exploration (Claim Checkers, Image Investigators, or Context Detectives). They identify moments where AI felt trustworthy and moments where confidence masked gaps or assumptions.
</p>

<p style="font-size: 13pt">
Students then reorganize into <strong>Jigsaw Groups</strong> to compare findings across explorations and identify universal credibility signals. The focus is on patterns and rules, not retelling steps.
</p>

<p style="font-size: 14pt"><b>🧾 Project Work</b></p>

<p style="font-size: 13pt">
Using evidence from their explorations and discussions, students design a visual <strong>Trust Meter</strong> that defines multiple levels of credibility (for example, “Highly Trustworthy” to “Falls Apart”).
</p>

<p style="font-size: 13pt">
Each level must clearly explain:
</p>

<ul style="font-size: 13pt; margin-top: 5px;">
<li>What makes an AI output land there</li>
<li>What evidence is present or missing</li>
<li>What specific user actions increase responsible use</li>
</ul>

<p style="font-size: 13pt">
Students then add their completed Trust Meter to their Responsible AI Use Guide project.
</p>

</div>
</details>

<!-- Wrap Up -->
<details style="background-color: #ececec; padding: 12px; margin-bottom: 15px; border-radius: 6px;">
<summary style="font-size: 13pt; font-weight: bold;">
Wrap Up (5 minutes)
</summary>
<div style="margin-top: 12px; font-size: 13pt; line-height: 1.4;">
<p style="font-size: 13pt">
Students reflect on how training data and representation affect credibility by discussing what happens when AI is trained on limited or unrepresentative examples.
</p>
<p style="font-size: 13pt">
Close by revisiting the guiding question: <em>How do you decide when to trust AI?</em> and reinforce that responsible use requires deliberate verification strategies.
</p>
</div>
</details>

### 🧾 **What to Prepare**:
* #### Ensure students bring their Milestone 3 Learning Log.
* #### Preview the Trust Meter project level and Sketch Lab tools.
* #### Prepare instructions for Expert and Jigsaw group transitions.
* #### Review expectations for including evidence-based reasoning in the Trust Meter.

### 💡 **Tips for Teaching**:
* #### Push students beyond vague advice like “check it.” Ask what specifically to look for.
* #### Encourage conditional thinking rather than absolute rules.
* #### Require that each Trust Meter level references a concrete example from testing.
MARKDOWN
